00:31:42.460 --> 00:31:43.615
that you guys made there.

00:31:43.615 --> 00:31:48.590
Sergey, you said, 2019 to
today, that's a big change.

00:31:48.590 --> 00:31:50.450
The scale is impressive.

00:31:50.450 --> 00:31:53.290
So tell us about the volumes
of data that you're seeing.

00:31:53.290 --> 00:31:54.310
How did it change?

00:31:54.310 --> 00:31:55.030
What happened?

00:31:55.030 --> 00:31:57.520
What's the impact?

00:31:57.520 --> 00:32:00.490
SERGEY CHERNYAKOV: As I said
in my presentation, in 2009,

00:32:00.490 --> 00:32:05.170
we had our [? MVP ?] built
on monolithic architecture.

00:32:05.170 --> 00:32:09.250
Like data processing,
dashboard database

00:32:09.250 --> 00:32:11.320
was on the same server.

00:32:11.320 --> 00:32:15.340
And as the business expanded,
we built a relational database

00:32:15.340 --> 00:32:18.520
cluster to store visitors' data.

00:32:18.520 --> 00:32:23.050
And we used this approach
for about two years.

00:32:23.050 --> 00:32:27.340
It's not perfect for our needs.

00:32:27.340 --> 00:32:32.410
So we constructed now a
scale data store architecture

00:32:32.410 --> 00:32:38.490
with Apache Spark
jobs in batch mode

00:32:38.490 --> 00:32:40.500
that we host on
virtual machines.

00:32:40.500 --> 00:32:43.970
And we administrate
ourselves, and it's

00:32:43.970 --> 00:32:47.600
very painful in
terms of maintenance.

00:32:47.600 --> 00:32:52.120
So one year ago, we migrated
over to Google and [INAUDIBLE]..

00:32:52.120 --> 00:32:54.500
REMI AUBERT: We had a
look yesterday with Sergey

00:32:54.500 --> 00:32:56.410
about the amount
of data we have.

00:32:56.410 --> 00:32:58.930
And we made a calculation
that every six months,

00:32:58.930 --> 00:33:01.400
we doubled the size of
the data storage we have.

00:33:01.400 --> 00:33:05.130
So the increase of volume
we ingest is very important.

00:33:05.130 --> 00:33:06.490
EVREN ERYUREK: Very important.

00:33:06.490 --> 00:33:07.090
Very good.

00:33:07.090 --> 00:33:13.090
So I highlighted several
times that batch and streaming

00:33:13.090 --> 00:33:16.690
and how having the same set
of code, same sort of tools

00:33:16.690 --> 00:33:17.890
is important.

00:33:17.890 --> 00:33:20.170
Can you share with
our guests in here

00:33:20.170 --> 00:33:23.830
what that impact was when
you used to do it with batch?

00:33:23.830 --> 00:33:26.290
Now, you're doing it
real time with streaming,

00:33:26.290 --> 00:33:28.540
and you didn't have to change
much of your code there.

00:33:28.540 --> 00:33:30.490
What was the impact
from business side,

00:33:30.490 --> 00:33:33.733
as well as from the
technology side?

00:33:33.733 --> 00:33:35.150
REMI AUBERT: From
a business side,

00:33:35.150 --> 00:33:39.560
it was more problems
than solution.

00:33:39.560 --> 00:33:41.780
When you do batch
and the time lapse,

00:33:41.780 --> 00:33:44.060
you have to process a batch.

00:33:44.060 --> 00:33:48.230
It's less than the time you
need to process the batch.

00:33:48.230 --> 00:33:50.100
At some point, you are stuck.

00:33:50.100 --> 00:33:53.900
So we had trouble to
compute that batch

00:33:53.900 --> 00:33:58.530
and to give good experience
for our own clients.

00:33:58.530 --> 00:34:00.800
So that's why we tried
to stream as much

00:34:00.800 --> 00:34:05.220
as we could all the
aspect of the tool.

00:34:05.220 --> 00:34:08.449
And in terms of personalization
that I showed you

00:34:08.449 --> 00:34:10.639
in the example, we cannot
do that with batch,

00:34:10.639 --> 00:34:15.110
as we have to be able to
categorize the user and give

00:34:15.110 --> 00:34:18.290
new user experience after
the first page [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:18.290 --> 00:34:19.230
the user.

00:34:19.230 --> 00:34:22.670
So in batch, we cannot do that,
as people will switch from

00:34:22.670 --> 00:34:26.030
the first page to the second
page in less than five seconds.

00:34:26.030 --> 00:34:28.530
So we have to calculate
that in real time.

00:34:28.530 --> 00:34:29.480
EVREN ERYUREK: I see.

00:34:29.480 --> 00:34:31.130
Anything from your perspective?

00:34:31.130 --> 00:34:34.940
SERGEY CHERNYAKOV: Yes, but
for the clients, I think, also,

00:34:34.940 --> 00:34:38.960
we cannot target our
visitors in real time based

00:34:38.960 --> 00:34:43.280
on their current behavior
when we use batch mode.

00:34:43.280 --> 00:34:44.080
It's complicated.

00:34:44.080 --> 00:34:48.590
And from a technical
point of view, for me,

00:34:48.590 --> 00:34:52.409
batch processing is more painful
to administrate, to [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:52.409 --> 00:34:54.889
or to manage jobs, scheduling.

00:34:54.889 --> 00:35:00.560
And it requires also more
computing resource, because we

00:35:00.560 --> 00:35:02.240
process much more data at once.

00:35:02.240 --> 00:35:02.990
EVREN ERYUREK: OK.

00:35:02.990 --> 00:35:04.070
OK, well, thank you.

